<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<appendix xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ap-intervals">
  <title>Intervals</title>
  <section xml:id="sec-intervals">
    <title>Intervals</title>
    <p>
      An <term>interval</term><idx><h>interval</h></idx> is a subset of
      <m>\R</m> of one of the following forms, where <m>a,b\in\R</m> and
      <m>a\le b</m>:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><m>(a,b):=\{x\in\R\colon a\lt x\lt b\}</m></li>
        <li><m>[a,b]:=\{x\in\R\colon a\le x\le b\}</m></li>
        <li><m>(a,b]:=\{x\in\R\colon a\lt x\le b\}</m></li>
        <li><m>[a,b):=\{x\in\R\colon a\le x\lt b\}</m></li>
        <li><m>(a,\infty):=\{x\in\R\colon a\lt x\}</m></li>
        <li><m>[a,\infty):=\{x\in\R\colon a\le x\}</m></li>
        <li><m>(-\infty,b):=\{x\in\R\colon x\lt b\}</m></li>
        <li><m>(-\infty,b]:=\{x\in\R\colon x\le b\}</m></li>
        <li><m>\R=(-\infty,\infty)</m></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      When <m>a=b</m>, the interval <m>(a,b)</m> is empty and
      <m>[a,b]=\{a\}</m>. A <term>nondegenerate interval</term> is an
      interval with at least two points.
    </p>
    <p>
      An interval contains every point between any two of its elements. The
      following proposition shows that this property characterizes intervals.
    </p>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-classification-of-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A subset <m>I</m> of <m>\R</m> is an interval if and only if, for
          every <m>x,y\in I</m> with <m>x\lt y</m>, we have
          <m>[x,y]\subseteq I</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Every interval listed above has the stated property. Conversely, let
          <m>I\subseteq\R</m> have this property. If <m>I=\emptyset</m>, then
          <m>I=(0,0)</m>; if <m>I=\{a\}</m>, then <m>I=[a,a]</m>. Thus, we may
          assume that <m>I</m> is nondegenerate.
        </p>
        <p>
          First suppose that <m>I</m> is bounded below but not above. Let
          <m>a=\inf I</m>. If <m>z&gt;a</m>, then <m>z</m> is not a lower bound
          of <m>I</m>, so there is <m>x\in I</m> with <m>x&lt;z</m>. Since
          <m>I</m> is not bounded above, there is also <m>y\in I</m> with
          <m>z&lt;y</m>. Since <m>x&lt;z&lt;y</m>, the assumed property gives
          <m>z\in I</m>. Thus <m>(a,\infty)\subseteq I</m>. On the other hand,
          no element of <m>I</m> can be less than <m>a</m>. Hence
          <m>I=(a,\infty)</m> or <m>I=[a,\infty)</m>, according as
          <m>a\notin I</m> or <m>a\in I</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The case in which <m>I</m> is bounded above but not below is similar:
          if <m>b=\sup I</m>, then <m>I=(-\infty,b)</m> or
          <m>I=(-\infty,b]</m>, according as <m>b\notin I</m> or <m>b\in I</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>I</m> is neither bounded below nor bounded above, then for every
          <m>z\in\R</m> we can choose <m>x,y\in I</m> with <m>x&lt;z&lt;y</m>.
          The assumed property gives <m>z\in I</m>, so <m>I=\R</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, suppose that <m>I</m> is bounded both below and above. Let
          <m>a=\inf I</m> and <m>b=\sup I</m>. Since <m>I</m> is nondegenerate,
          we have <m>a&lt;b</m>. If <m>a&lt;z&lt;b</m>, then <m>z</m> is neither a
          lower bound nor an upper bound of <m>I</m>. Thus, there exist
          <m>x,y\in I</m> with <m>x&lt;z&lt;y</m>, and hence <m>z\in I</m>.
          Therefore <m>(a,b)\subseteq I</m>. Also, every element of <m>I</m>
          lies in <m>[a,b]</m>. Consequently, <m>I</m> is one of
          <m>(a,b)</m>, <m>[a,b]</m>, <m>(a,b]</m>, or <m>[a,b)</m>, according
          to whether <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> belong to <m>I</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-interval-cardinality-r">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Every nondegenerate interval <m>I\subseteq\R</m> has cardinality
          <m>|\R|</m>. In particular, it is uncountable.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Choose <m>a,b\in I</m> with <m>a&lt;b</m>. By
          <xref ref="prop-classification-of-intervals"/>,
          <m>(a,b)\subseteq I\subseteq\R</m>. The map
          <m>x\mapsto(x-c)/r</m>, where <m>c=(a+b)/2</m> and <m>r=(b-a)/2</m>,
          is a bijection <m>(a,b)\to(-1,1)</m>. Also,
          <m>t\mapsto\tan(\pi t/2)</m> is a bijection <m>(-1,1)\to\R</m>.
          Hence <m>|\R|=|(a,b)|\le|I|\le|\R|</m>, so <m>|I|=|\R|</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-intersection-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>The intersection of two intervals is an interval.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>I,J</m> be intervals. If <m>I\cap J</m> has fewer than two
          points, then it is an interval. Otherwise, let <m>x,y\in I\cap J</m>
          with <m>x&lt;y</m>. Since <m>I</m> and <m>J</m> are intervals,
          <m>[x,y]\subseteq I</m> and <m>[x,y]\subseteq J</m>. Thus,
          <m>[x,y]\subseteq I\cap J</m>, and
          <xref ref="prop-classification-of-intervals"/> shows that
          <m>I\cap J</m> is an interval.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
    <proposition xml:id="prop-union-overlapping-intervals">
      <statement>
        <p>The union of two overlapping intervals is an interval.</p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Let <m>I,J</m> be overlapping intervals, and choose <m>a\in I\cap J</m>.
          Let <m>x,y\in I\cup J</m> with <m>x&lt;y</m>. If <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>
          belong to the same one of the intervals, then <m>[x,y]</m> is contained
          in that interval and hence in <m>I\cup J</m>. Otherwise, after
          relabeling, we may assume that <m>x\in I\setminus J</m> and
          <m>y\in J\setminus I</m>. If <m>a&lt;x</m>, then
          <m>x\in[a,y]\subseteq J</m>, a contradiction. If <m>y&lt;a</m>, then
          <m>y\in[x,a]\subseteq I</m>, also a contradiction. Therefore,
          <m>x\le a\le y</m>. Consequently,
          <m>[x,y]=[x,a]\cup[a,y]\subseteq I\cup J</m>. By
          <xref ref="prop-classification-of-intervals"/>, <m>I\cup J</m> is an
          interval.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </proposition>
  </section>
</appendix>
